---
name: nadia-gate-check
version: 0.1.0
description: Runs the gate's contract rules (SPEC §3.1) and exits non-zero if any of them changed.
---

# Contract check for `nadia.gate`

The Rust and Scala 3 implementations assert these in their unit-test suites. This side has no
test harness in this repository (a gap recorded in `BACKLOG.md`, not invented here), so the
same assertions run as a script:

```bash
ssc run src/gate-check.ssc          # exit 0 = the contract holds
```

Only the rules `SPEC.md` §3.1 makes **contract** are checked — the ones where the three
implementations disagreeing means one of them is a bug. Wording and rounds are policy and
belong to each front-end.

[unquote, cargoRunFragment, cargoRunFragmentArgs, shellLex, taskArgvFor, taskStates, isHallucinatedCargoCheck, misplacedProject, cargoFloor, runCheck](gate.ssc)
[exit](std/os.ssc)
[readFile, exists](std/fs.ssc)
[jsonRead](std/json.ssc)

```scalascript
var failures = 0

def check(name: String, ok: Boolean): Unit =
  if ok then println("  ok   " + name)
  else
    println("  FAIL " + name)
    failures = failures + 1

def eq(name: String, got: String, want: String): Unit =
  check(name + " (got `" + got + "`, want `" + want + "`)", got == want)

println("nadia.gate — contract check (SPEC §3.1)")

// A delimiting quote is not part of the argument. The measured case: a task writes
// `cargo run -- "3 4 + 2 *"` and the model returns the argument the way the task spelled it.
eq("one symmetric pair comes off", unquote("\"3 4 + 2 *\""), "3 4 + 2 *")
eq("single quotes too", unquote("'y'"), "y")
eq("an empty expectation survives", unquote("\"\""), "")
eq("exactly one pair, not all", unquote("'\"x\"'"), "\"x\"")
// What must NOT be stripped, because there the quote is data.
eq("plain text untouched", unquote("hello"), "hello")
eq("unbalanced is data", unquote("\"unbalanced"), "\"unbalanced")
eq("internal quotes are data", unquote("he said \"hi\""), "he said \"hi\"")
eq("quotes around parts are data", unquote("\"a\" + \"b\""), "\"a\" + \"b\"")

// The fragment quotes the model's values and SAYS what it saw on a mismatch.
val frag = cargoRunFragment("\"3 4 + 2 *\"", "\"14\"")
check("fragment carries the value, not the spelling", frag.contains("'3 4 + 2 *'"))
check("fragment carries the expectation", frag.contains("'14'"))
check("fragment prints both on a mismatch", frag.contains("printed") && frag.contains("expected"))

// Arity is part of the criterion. Found END TO END on 2026-08-04, not by review: for the task
// "cargo run -- 3 4 must print 7" the model answered with both numbers in one string, the check
// ran `cargo run -q -- '3 4'` against a correct two-argument program, and the gate reported
// FAILED after spending both repair rounds. `cargo run -- 3 4` printed 7 by hand.
val two = cargoRunFragmentArgs(List("3", "4"), "7")
check("two arguments stay two", two.contains("cargo run -q -- '3' '4'"))
val one = cargoRunFragmentArgs(List("3 4 + 2 *"), "14")
check("one argument with spaces stays one", one.contains("cargo run -q -- '3 4 + 2 *'"))
check("the message names the whole command line", two.contains("'3 4'"))

// Arity comes from the TASK, not from the model — measured in both directions on 2026-08-04.
val twoTask = "print the sum of two arguments: cargo run -- 3 4 must print 7"
val oneTask = "an RPN calculator: cargo run -- \"3 4 + 2 *\" must print 14"
eq("the task's two arguments win", taskArgvFor(twoTask, "3 4").getOrElse(List()).mkString("|"), "3|4")
eq("the task's ONE quoted argument wins", taskArgvFor(oneTask, "3 4 + 2 *").getOrElse(List()).mkString("|"), "3 4 + 2 *")
check("a value the task never states is left alone", taskArgvFor(twoTask, "5 6").isEmpty)
eq("the lexer groups what the quotes group", shellLex("\"3 4 + 2 *\" must print 14").mkString("|"), "3 4 + 2 *|must|print|14")

// An expectation the task never states is the model inventing the answer — measured 2026-08-05
// on `wordcount`, where the derived check demanded three lines that appear nowhere and no correct
// program could pass it (0/4 before the guard, 3/3 after).
val wcTask = "read a text file, count words case-insensitively and print the top 3 as `word count`"
check("an invented expectation is not a criterion", !taskStates(wcTask, "a 3 c 2 d 2"))
val revTask = "fix the bug: `cargo run -- hello` must print exactly `olleh`"
check("what the task DOES state is checked", taskStates(revTask, "olleh"))
check("case and spacing do not matter", taskStates(revTask, "  OLLEH "))
check("an empty expectation is not looked up", !taskStates(revTask, ""))

// An invented cargo check is recognized: no manifest, and a task that never asked for Rust.
check("invented cargo check is dropped",
      isHallucinatedCargoCheck("cargo run -q -- pong", "/tmp/nadia-gate-check-nonexistent", "reply with pong"))
check("a task that DOES ask for Rust keeps its check",
      !isHallucinatedCargoCheck("cargo build -q", "/tmp/nadia-gate-check-nonexistent",
                                "write a Rust program that prints pong"))

// A workspace with no cargo project has no floor to stand on.
check("no floor without a manifest", cargoFloor("/tmp/nadia-gate-check-nonexistent").isEmpty)

// A project one level down is NAMED (the repair prompt uses this); ambiguity says nothing.
check("nothing to report for a directory that does not exist",
      misplacedProject("/tmp/nadia-gate-check-nonexistent").isEmpty)

// The two that shell out. The pure rules above all passed while `runCheck` was calling `exec`
// with the wrong arity — a defect only a real call could show, and it took a live run to find.
val (okTrue, _) = runCheck("true", "/tmp")
check("a passing command reads as passed", okTrue)
val (okFalse, out) = runCheck("echo hello; exit 1", "/tmp")
check("a failing command reads as failed", !okFalse)
check("and the repair round gets what it printed", out.contains("hello"))

// ── The shared corpus ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `contract/gate-cases.json` is read by all three implementations (SPEC §3.1). The assertions above
// are this file's own; these are the ones every leg answers, so a rule added once is checked three
// times. The file is found relative to the repository root, which is where `ssc run` is invoked.
val corpusPath = "contract/gate-cases.json"
if !exists(corpusPath) then
  println("")
  println("MISSING " + corpusPath + " — the corpus IS the contract; run this from the repo root")
  exit(1)

val corpus = jsonRead(readFile(corpusPath))

for c <- corpus.get("shell_lex").asList do
  val got = shellLex(c.get("in").asString)
  val want = c.get("out").asList.map(v => v.asString).toList
  check("corpus shell_lex: " + c.get("why").asString, got == want)

for c <- corpus.get("task_states").asList do
  val got = taskStates(c.get("task").asString, c.get("expect").asString)
  check("corpus task_states: " + c.get("why").asString, got == c.get("out").asBool)

for c <- corpus.get("task_argv_for").asList do
  val got = taskArgvFor(c.get("task").asString, c.get("joined").asString)
  val out = c.get("out")
  val want = if out.isNull then None else Some(out.asList.map(v => v.asString).toList)
  check("corpus task_argv_for: " + c.get("why").asString, got == want)

println("")
if failures == 0 then
  println("contract holds")
  exit(0)
else
  println(failures.toString + " rule(s) changed — SPEC §3.1 and this file disagree, and one of them is a bug")
  exit(1)
```
